@@ -127,6 +127,30 @@ slop-deps --strict
 slop-deps --help
 ```
 
+## Security
+
+The local dashboard (`--ui`) is hardened against remote attack — verified by an
+adversarial red-team pass:
+
+- **Crash-proof request handling** — malformed request targets (`http://[`,
+  oversized URLs, ...) answer `400`; a single crafted packet can never kill the
+  process via an unhandled rejection.
+- **DNS-rebinding guard** — only `Host: 127.0.0.1:<port>` / `localhost:<port>`
+  requests are honored; foreign Host headers get `403`.
+- **Symlink containment** — `?dir=` cannot point the scanner outside the
+  dashboard root through a symlink; real paths are re-checked after resolution.
+- **Path traversal blocked** — `?dir=../..` and encoded variants get `400`.
+
+Two trust boundaries worth knowing about:
+
+- **`.slopdepsrc.json` is opt-in** (`--trust-config`). A repository under scan
+  can never mark its own typosquats as builtins/popular to dodge verdicts —
+  the config only loads when you explicitly trust it.
+- **`--fix` is injection-guarded.** The BYOK AI may only rewrite the *module
+  specifier* of a flagged import line; rewrites that smuggle statements or
+  arbitrary code (`import x; require('child_process')...`) are rejected
+  outright and the original line is left untouched.
+
 ## Privacy
 
 **No telemetry. No accounts. No data collection.** A scan's only network traffic is GET lookups of package names against registry.npmjs.org and pypi.org — the same requests any package manager sends. Full breakdown in [PRIVACY.md](PRIVACY.md) — don't take my word for it, run `grep -rn "fetch(" src/` and verify. Two commands for full control:
